---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Can Chrome block a specific website by itself?',
    a: 'For an ordinary personal Chrome profile, a focused browser extension is the most direct way to maintain a custom blocklist. Managed organizations can also enforce URL policies administratively.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does a site that was already open stay visible?',
    a: 'The page has already loaded. Reload it or navigate again so the new blocking rule can apply. Retroactively inspecting every open tab would require broader tab or history access.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I enter the full URL?',
    a: 'For torwache, enter the domain, such as youtube.com, without https:// or a page path. Enable subdomains if you also want addresses such as music.youtube.com covered.',
  },
];

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to block websites on Chrome',
    description: 'Add a distracting domain to a local Chrome website blocklist in four steps.',
    totalTime: 'PT2M',
    step: [
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: 'Install a website blocker extension' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: 'Open its blocklist' },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 3,
        name: 'Add the domain and choose subdomain coverage',
      },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: 'Reload any tab that was already open' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map(({ q, a }) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="How to block websites on Chrome"
  metaTitle="How to block websites on Chrome"
  description="Learn how to block distracting websites on Chrome with a simple domain list, including subdomains, existing tabs, permissions, and privacy checks."
  eyebrow="Two-minute guide"
  intro="The simplest method is a small Chrome extension with a domain blocklist. Add the site once, decide whether its subdomains count, and let Chrome refuse future navigations."
  updated="August 24, 2026"
  structuredData={structuredData}
  toc={[
    { href: '#quick-method', label: 'The quick method' },
    { href: '#example', label: 'A concrete example' },
    { href: '#other-methods', label: 'Other blocking methods' },
    { href: '#privacy', label: 'Check the privacy cost' },
    { href: '#questions', label: 'Common questions' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="quick-method">
    <h2>The quick method</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span>1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Install a website blocker extension</h3>
          <p>
            Prefer one whose store listing explains every permission and whose privacy policy
            distinguishes extension data from website analytics.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Open the blocklist</h3>
          <p>
            A blocker should make its central action obvious. In torwache, the entire interface
            is the list—there is no dashboard to configure first.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Add the domain</h3>
          <p>
            Enter <code>reddit.com</code>, not a search phrase or a full URL. Turn on subdomain
            blocking when you want addresses below that domain covered too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>4</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Reload anything already open</h3>
          <p>
            Blocking affects the next request or navigation. A page that has already loaded
            will remain until it reloads or navigates.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="example">
    <h2>Example: block YouTube without overcomplicating it</h2>
    <p>
      Add <code>youtube.com</code> and enable subdomains. New attempts to open YouTube are sent
      to a local blocked page. When you genuinely want access again, return to the list and
      switch the entry off.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>No “five more minutes” button</strong>
      <p>
        A convenient bypass turns a block page into another decision. torwache deliberately
        makes you edit the list instead.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="other-methods">
    <h2>When an extension is not the right tool</h2>
    <p>
      A Chrome extension is ideal when the distraction lives in Chrome and you control the
      browser profile. Use something else when the boundary needs to extend further:
    </p>
    <dl class="decision-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Router or DNS filtering</dt>
        <dd>Useful when the same domains should be unavailable to multiple devices.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Operating-system blocker</dt>
        <dd>Better when desktop applications and every installed browser must be covered.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Managed Chrome policy</dt>
        <dd>Appropriate for schools or organizations administering many browser profiles.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Check what the blocker receives in return</h2>
    <p>
      Website blockers sit unusually close to browsing behavior. Before installing one, read
      the permission prompt and its store privacy disclosures. Look for plain answers to four
      questions:
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Can it read or change the content of every page?</li>
      <li>Does your blocklist stay on the device or sync to an account?</li>
      <li>Does it record website usage to produce charts or streaks?</li>
      <li>Does the extension make network requests of its own?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      torwache uses local storage and Chrome’s declarative network rules. It has no content
      scripts or host permissions, loads no remote assets, and makes no background network
      requests. External links open only when clicked. The exact permission rationale is published
      on the <a href="/#permissions">homepage</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="questions">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <dl class="faq compact-faq">
      {faq.map(({ q, a }) => <div><dt>{q}</dt><dd>{a}</dd></div>)}
    </dl>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
